      "explanation": "<img src=\"https://imagex.cdn.examgoal.net/0eWJ1GR4NlPlHtCAA/JcKpG9j1HxOTKnxTDPRT5phkqwjw5/4htZqKPCHa5BflRbaAGghq/image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%;  height: auto;display: block;margin: 0 auto;\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"JEE Main 2019 (Online) 12th January Evening Slot Chemistry - Haloalkanes and Haloarenes Question 151 English Explanation 1\">\n<br>As here HBr is present in excess, so HBr will react to alkene and  ether part sinultaneously. \n<br><br>According to Markovnikov rule. Carbocation close to benzene ring is more stable. \n<br><br>In the given reagent, carbocation is <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.262ex\" height=\"1.181ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -511 1000 522\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"2192\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2192\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container>\n<br><br><img src=\"https://imagex.cdn.examgoal.net/uMEPgtUUGX18RMchK/GNTt0kIoEkHFsiscqq58S410cQRF1/3Ggm25ISZHjWNbVWkopfsy/image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%;  height: auto;display: block;margin: 0 auto;\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"JEE Main 2019 (Online) 12th January Evening Slot Chemistry - Haloalkanes and Haloarenes Question 151 English Explanation 2\">\n<br>So in HBr, Br<sup><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.186ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.76ex\" height=\"1.505ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -583 778 665\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"2212\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2212\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container></sup> adds to the carbocation.\n<br><br>And in the ether part because of lone pair of oxygen, it will take part in resonance a double bond will create between O atom and benzene ring. So, this bond will be stronger. That is why CH<sub>3</sub> <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.186ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.76ex\" height=\"1.505ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -583 778 665\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"2212\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2212\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> O bond will break."
